MAJOR_VERSION=5
STANDARD_VERSION=3
RELEASE_VERSION=0
PRERELEASE=1
DEV_VERSION=12
DESCRIPTION=KeyQuorum pin, ABI and first consumers. third_party/keyquorum moves from 8ab454b (ABI 3) to KeyQuorum merged main 1fa6aab, the merge commit of KeyQuorum pull request #187 (ABI 5), which carries the merged KeyQuorum #180 work (the store-free kind-17 snapshot open and the kq_file_seal_snapshot, kq_file_open_snapshot and kq_file_snapshot_covers exports, kq_slot_open, kq_bridge_prove, kq_bridge_verify, kq_bridge_connects, kq_access_requirement, KQER error reports) and kq_file_head_author. FL_KQ_ABI_VERSION in contracts/drivers/contract_p4_keyquorum.h is 5, so the driver probe (kq_probe) still requires equality with the linked archive, and docs/FILE_SHARING_KEYQUORUM.md states the ABI 5 probe. test_kq_net gains test_a_refused_ack_is_recorded_as_refused_over_the_wire: a refused ACK is recorded as refused, never as delivered. Consumers: the driver gains a head_author op over kq_file_head_author (hosted builds only; the freestanding image, which has no checkpoint storage, keeps its size), fl_kq_cp_head_author names a tracked file's single head author, and fl_kq_autosign_head_author is the autosign job's callback, so a forked file is audited as kq-autosign-forked and not signed (server file send -t now registers the file it made a history for, and the periodic job signs it with the author's key through kq_file_check_in, adding one signed revision per sweep; fl_kq_service_autosign_remove stops it). A refused receive signs a KQER report as the online account and writes it as a .kqer sidecar in the held landing directory (fl_kq_report_refusal_in; nothing is written and kq-report-offline is audited when no account is online, and fl_kq_report_refusal, which holds no directory, writes nothing and audits kq-report-uncontained), and fl_kq_cp_inject_report_with verifies a held report and appends one ErrorReported event to the file's history; the report is written only through the held landing directory, at most 64 per directory (a ledger, reports.kqer; kq-report-capped past it), and the recipient sends it after the refusing ACK on a new session opcode, FL_NET_SESSION_OP_KQR_REPORT (0x52), which the host forwards once to the delivery's sender, where server file reports records it with fl_kq_cp_inject_report_bytes. Login, su and sudo: the driver gains access_requirement, bridge_prove, bridge_verify and bridge_connects ops (compiled out of the freestanding image, where a missing op refuses), fl_kq_unlock_requirement asks KeyQuorum which factor a switch needs from the store's own live bridges, and fl_session_switch_user enforces it: a token factor opens the holder's KQST, a bridge factor adds a member's proof verified against the store's current bridge facts and re-checked before the commit, and a parent-grant requirement, a store that cannot be read or a malformed live bridge refuses. Password-only accounts keep their earlier password behaviour where the rule names a token they do not hold, audited as su-kqst-legacy or sudo-kqst-legacy. account_bridge_members gains wrap_salt beside the sealed bridge key; bridge kq-import copies a KeyQuorum private bridge from KeyQuorum's own store into the account store in one transaction (fl_user_db_bridge_import_from_kq). test_kq_real_token (its own target, skipped without the keyquorum CLI) opens real slot tokens and proves a real private bridge. FL-04: the native shell installs the history gate and source for the deliveries it sent (the requester must be the delivery's recipient, then FL_AUTHZ_OP_FILE_HISTORY_EXPORT), signed by the online account. FL-14: the freestanding identity takes an optional KeyQuorum ops table, installed by the WebAssembly lab before its demo accounts are seeded, so a new account's signing key and box key are sealed with the driver's account_seal and the password is not kept; login, su and sudo open that seal. switchuser stays password-free until that restriction is confirmed. The QEMU image installs a slimmer table (kq_identity.c; not the security-lab image, which has no room) and takes its entropy from RDRAND or from a launcher-supplied fw_cfg file (kernel/drivers/freestanding_x86_64/fwcfg.c), failing closed without either, while the public image keeps its demo accounts. The refused-ACK wire test also checks that the host deletes the letter file. fl_session_remove_user refuses, under the session lock, to delete the account the session is logged in as. A failed fl_session_reload_users drops that name, the elevation token, and the sudo scope, and starts a new login generation, so fl_session_has_elevation does not keep the old authority. sudo grants its elevation token under the session lock only while the login that was authenticated is still current. su and sudo ask FL_AUTHZ_OP_IDENTITY_SWITCH before a password prompt, including sudo of another command. A private bridge generation is accepted only as an integer from 1 through UINT32_MAX; a fractional, text, NULL, or out-of-range value is not a live bridge. Test links that include authz_subsystem.o also link audit_log.o, which defines fl_audit_authz_event. sudo rechecks Root's auth mode and the password that was accepted, under the session lock, before it grants elevation. FL-15: a KQST login also publishes its box secret to the key service (fl_session_set_login_box_hook), kept for the session and handed only to the account's own label (fl_kq_obtain_box_key). FL-20: fl_kq_service_set_root_credential_provider lets each runtime supply Root's fallback credential; none is stored by default. FL-02 stage 2: kq_mem_storage.c is a binary in-memory fl_kq_storage_t whose dir_commit replaces all files or none, tested with the real checkpoints; it is not linked into the image yet. The bridge command's batch token count covers kq-import.
